WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.586 --> 00:00:12.759
In the late 560s, a Germanic group
known as the Lombards

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.678
was wreaking havoc through northern Italy.

00:00:15.845 --> 00:00:21.517
To escape the invaders, boatloads
of people fled into the Venetian Lagoon,

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:25.646
a morass of swampy mudflats
and scattered islands.

00:00:25.855 --> 00:00:32.195
Formed over centuries by the outflow
of silt from the Po and Piave Rivers

00:00:32.361 --> 00:00:35.531
and the counterforce of Adriatic currents,

00:00:35.656 --> 00:00:40.495
this marshy maze was constantly
shifting and disappearing.

00:00:40.828 --> 00:00:44.540
These qualities made it
both the perfect spot to hide

00:00:44.832 --> 00:00:48.127
and a very unstable place to live.

00:00:48.836 --> 00:00:52.590
So the new arrivals set
out to tame the lagoon

00:00:52.799 --> 00:00:57.345
and build the most unlikely of cities
from the mud.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:02.642
While some fishermen already
inhabited the region in stilted huts,

00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:05.019
the settlers wanted to build a foundation

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:08.606
to support heavier,
longer lasting structures.

00:01:08.606 --> 00:01:14.028
Little did they know, their work
would support Venice to this very day.

00:01:14.487 --> 00:01:20.535
They drove clusters of 1 to 3-meter-long
wooden piles into the ground,

00:01:20.535 --> 00:01:26.958
in a process that squeezed out groundwater
and compacted mud around the piles.

00:01:27.625 --> 00:01:31.796
This created an oxygen-free environment
that protected the wood

00:01:31.796 --> 00:01:33.798
from insects and fungi.

00:01:34.632 --> 00:01:38.386
The piles were still vulnerable
to minor bacterial damage,

00:01:38.594 --> 00:01:41.347
but otherwise impervious to rot.

00:01:41.556 --> 00:01:46.519
And this sealed system of wood,
water, and mud

00:01:46.519 --> 00:01:52.400
is still supporting Venice
1,500 years later.

00:01:53.192 --> 00:01:56.988
Wooden beams and stone platforms
above the piles

00:01:56.988 --> 00:01:59.282
provided the bases for buildings.

00:01:59.448 --> 00:02:02.201
And sometime around 697,

00:02:02.326 --> 00:02:07.331
the settlers formally declared
their new home the Republic of Venice.

00:02:07.915 --> 00:02:12.044
Initially, footbridges were built
to connect the islands,

00:02:12.044 --> 00:02:16.340
but these were prone to decay,
collapse, and fire.

00:02:16.465 --> 00:02:21.012
Many, like the famed Rialto Bridge,
had to be regularly rebuilt,

00:02:21.262 --> 00:02:24.932
each time incorporating new materials
and techniques

00:02:24.932 --> 00:02:26.684
to improve their longevity.

00:02:27.059 --> 00:02:31.939
By the 11th century, the channels
between islands had been reinforced

00:02:31.939 --> 00:02:37.403
into stone-lined canals
navigated by slender gondolas.

00:02:37.862 --> 00:02:42.700
And unlike the rest of Europe, where
nobles typically rode above commoners,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:47.788
the city's layout necessitated
foot travel for all classes.

00:02:47.997 --> 00:02:50.333
Yet despite this clever engineering,

00:02:50.374 --> 00:02:53.252
the Republic still had no land
for farming,

00:02:53.586 --> 00:02:56.797
so Venetians were
heavily dependent on trade.

00:02:57.215 --> 00:03:02.136
Fortunately, Venice's location
made it the perfect port city

00:03:02.136 --> 00:03:05.806
to connect markets from Europe
to the Silk Road.

00:03:06.098 --> 00:03:10.978
Its canal network allowed boats
to dock close to warehouses,

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:14.732
and the challenge of navigating
its narrow waterways

00:03:14.732 --> 00:03:17.318
protected the city from outsiders.

00:03:17.693 --> 00:03:22.698
These advantages helped make
Venice a major maritime power,

00:03:22.698 --> 00:03:27.578
and the 1200s began
with their biggest business deal yet.

00:03:27.954 --> 00:03:32.083
The Pope brokered an arrangement
with Venice’s elected leader,

00:03:32.083 --> 00:03:33.793
Enrico Dandolo,

00:03:33.793 --> 00:03:35.836
to produce ships and arms

00:03:35.836 --> 00:03:41.425
for the Fourth Crusade’s
anticipated 33,000 soldiers.

00:03:41.634 --> 00:03:48.140
Dandolo agreed, investing huge amounts
of the republic’s money and resources.

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:54.146
But when it was time to set sail,
only one-third of the soldiers arrived,

00:03:54.272 --> 00:03:57.525
with less than half
of the promised payment.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:03.114
Furious, Dandolo redirected
the crusading army

00:04:03.114 --> 00:04:08.494
against Venice’s Christian rivals,
including Constantinople.

00:04:08.577 --> 00:04:13.374
This campaign laid the foundation
for Venice's overseas empire,

00:04:13.374 --> 00:04:15.293
and over the following centuries,

00:04:15.293 --> 00:04:20.131
Venetian merchants like Marco Polo
ventured as far as China.

00:04:20.423 --> 00:04:26.220
During the Renaissance, the republic
became a hub of art and intellectual life.

00:04:26.345 --> 00:04:31.684
Its sail-making looms were repurposed
to create vast canvases,

00:04:31.767 --> 00:04:35.396
its craftsmen pioneered
various printing techniques,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:40.443
and the silica-rich sands
from the Ticino and Adige rivers

00:04:40.443 --> 00:04:43.195
fueled extravagant glassworks.

00:04:43.612 --> 00:04:48.451
However, in addition
to art, silks, and spices,

00:04:48.743 --> 00:04:55.249
the Venetians also traded enslaved peoples
to work in private homes or on ships.

00:04:55.666 --> 00:05:01.505
Once freed, some stayed in the city,
even finding work as gondoliers.

00:05:01.964 --> 00:05:06.093
Venice's dominance over the Mediterranean
remained unchallenged

00:05:06.093 --> 00:05:08.346
until the mid-15th century,

00:05:08.346 --> 00:05:11.015
when the Ottomans
conquered Constantinople.

00:05:11.307 --> 00:05:12.850
In the following centuries,

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:17.104
the Ottomans used their navy
to interfere with Venice's business,

00:05:17.271 --> 00:05:22.735
all while new transoceanic
trade routes excluded Venetians.

00:05:23.486 --> 00:05:30.493
The republic soldiered on until French
attacks forced it to dissolve in 1797,

00:05:30.785 --> 00:05:36.082
at which point Venice traded hands
between Austria and France several times

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:41.253
before finally being ceded
to Italy in 1866.

00:05:41.712 --> 00:05:44.465
No longer able
to compete commercially,

00:05:44.548 --> 00:05:48.677
Venice was reinvented
as a tourist destination.

00:05:48.886 --> 00:05:52.598
But today, rising sea levels
are threatening to sink

00:05:52.598 --> 00:05:55.184
the so-called “floating city.”

00:05:55.267 --> 00:06:00.773
In 1900, the then 900-year-old
Basilica di San Marco

00:06:00.773 --> 00:06:03.150
flooded about seven times a year—

00:06:03.359 --> 00:06:08.697
now it experiences
roughly 250 annual floods.

00:06:09.073 --> 00:06:12.576
And tourists are perhaps
just as dangerous.

00:06:12.785 --> 00:06:16.831
As millions of annual visitors
speed along in motorboats,

00:06:16.914 --> 00:06:21.210
they erode the muddy barrier
protecting Venice’s foundations.

00:06:21.335 --> 00:06:22.878
Left unaddressed,

00:06:22.878 --> 00:06:29.677
these modern advances could destroy
one of the world’s most remarkable cities.
